BUG (found live 2026-07-21 by home-work-comms keeper): strand-ps is NOT installed on disk, so strand liveness detection is broken — and it caused a real duplicate-session near-miss on a live cluster.

Symptoms:
- `/home/peter/super/bin/strand-ps` -> No such file or directory (exit 127). `which strand-ps` empty. Not found anywhere under super/ or dotfiles/.
- Yet `cld` calls `$BIN/strand-ps` (this is exactly the "$BIN-relative trap" PR #5's method-graduation doc flagged: cld -> $BIN/strand-ps, graduate the cld/strand-ps/forkterm cluster together or switch to PATH lookups).
- Consequence: a strand can look NOT-LIVE (no .tty marker, dropped from `strands` LIVE list) while its claude process is still very much alive. Today aifabric-sessions' 10h session (PID 3286638) had shed its .tty and read as dead; a forkterm was launched into the strand on that false reading, producing TWO live aifabric-sessions sessions racing on the same authorised OSD-password rotation against a live green cluster. Caught before either acted (no /osd/* secret written, old pw still authed); old session SIGTERM'd clean. But the liveness gap is the root cause.

What to fix (aifabric owns cld/strand-ps/forkterm):
1. Ship strand-ps — it's referenced but absent. Restore/write it and put it where cld's $BIN resolves (or switch cld to a PATH lookup, per the PR#5 trap note).
2. Make liveness authoritative from PROCESS state, not just the .tty marker: a .tty file can be shed/stale while the process lives (see ding waiter lifecycle work). strand-ps should reconcile pts + /proc/<pid>/cwd against the strand dir, not trust .tty alone.
3. Consider a guard in `forkterm into <strand>` / cld: if a live claude already has cwd == that strand dir, warn before launching a second (dup-session guard), the same way ding/cld reap orphans.

Boundary note (keeper): none of this is comms-method — pure aifabric tooling. Handing it to you because you own the cluster and the cld/strand-ps/forkterm cluster; recording it so the near-miss isn't lost.
